  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nIsolated showers and storms have developed along and just west \nof I-81 this afternoon. We have enough surface heating to break \nany low level cap that was in place, and with CAPE values around\n1000-1500 j/kg, isolated thunderstorms are developing. Shear \nvalues are very low so storm organization is not really expected\nand these should be pulse type storms that develop and \ndissipated quickly, with additional cells building along outflow\nboundaries and areas of higher CAPE. A few of these storms have\ngrown tall enough to warrant a severe thunderstorm warning, \nwith the threat of a collapsing core pushing strong winds to the\nsurface. Some small hail may be possible if an updraft gets \ntall enough, but chances for this are low. \n\nStorm motion should be mostly from the NNW, but a few deviant\nmotions may occur as cells develop along outflow boundaries.\nThese motions will also be slow, which could produce isolated\ninstances of flash flooding, especially across urban areas \nand/or those areas prone to flooding. \n\nStorms should dissipate in the evening as we loose daytime\nheating. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA very strong upper level trough will dig over the Northeast \ntonight into tomorrow. This trough is expected to cutoff by late\nTuesday afternoon and it over much of the northeast through \nmidweek. A strong surface low will develop as the upper levels\ncutoff, making the weather pattern look much more like we are in\nthe fall instead of the end of July. Some uncertainty remains as\nto how the low will progress, with some solutions having a\nsingle center low meander from N to S through CNY on Tuesday \nthen hang out over western New England on Wednesday. Other\nsolutions have a double low forming, with energy transfered to\na low off the NJ coast that moves NE into eastern New England.\nCurrent thinking is that the single low solution is more likely,\nwhich brings multiple rounds of moderate to heavy rain to the\nregion, starting tonight through Wednesday. \n\nA widespread 1.5 to 2.5 inches is expected across much of the\narea from tonight through Wednesday. Atmospheric dynamics kick\nit up a notch over the Catskills and into the Poconos where a\nstrong LLJ is expected to develop on over the Hudson Valley that\nwill tap into and pull enhanced moisture from the Atlantic into\nthe region. Multiple models show signals that somewhere over \nthis area east into southern New England could see large\nrainfall amounts in the 4-5 inch range, with isolated 6in+ \npossible. The majority of this heavy rain is expected Tuesday\nafternoon into Wednesday morning. This conveyor belt of moisture\nis progged to wrap around the center of the low, bringing a\ndeformation bands on the NW side of the low as it moves into New\nEngland. This would bring chances for moderate rain showers\nacross the area through Wednesday. \n\nA Flash Flood Watch has been issued for Sullivan and Pike \ncounties from 11am Tuesday to 8pm Wednesday. Upslope flow \ncombined with the enhanced dynamics could bring impactful \nrainfall amounts to this area. A Slight Risk for Excessive\nRainfall leading to Flash Flooding is present from Otsego county\nS to far eastern portions of Luzerne. A Moderate risk touches\nportions of Sullivan county, where upslope enhancement should\nprovide the heaviest rainfall. The rest of the area is under a\nMarginal risk given the multiple periods of heavy rain could\ncause isolated flash flooding if an area is hit multiple times. \n\nThe Flash Flood risk should start to dissipate Wednesday\nafternoon as we loose a lot of the convective nature of the\nrainfall. \n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nThe upper low and surface low will be pretty stacked\nand is forecast to hang out over southern New England into\nThursday thru Friday, which will push scattered rain showers \nacross the area from the north. The low will push east by\nSaturday. The weekend forecast is still uncertain as guidance\nhandles a shortwave dropping into the Great Lakes differently.\nThe shortwave could start moving eastward by Saturday morning,\npushing a warm front into the area and generate showers along\nit, or it could dive farther south, get cutoff from the \neasterly flow and not impact us on Saturday. Either way, rain\nshowers could develop on Sunday as another trough from Canada\ndigs into the region.",
